    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I've implemented `fill_gaps` in `app/gaps.py`. The existing tests pass (`pytest -q`: 2 passed), and a separate check script I wrote also passed.\n\n**How it works:**\n- It works on a copy of `readings`, so the input frame isn't changed.\n- It groups rows by `station` and computes two things per station: the most recent earlier temperature and the first later one.\n- Each missing `temp_c` gets the earlier value if there is one, otherwise the later one. Values that were already present are never replaced.\n- Because it only looks within each station's group, it never uses another station's values. A station with no temperatures at all keeps its gaps, and so does a row with no `station` value.\n- The results are matched back to rows by position, not by index label, so it still works if the frame has repeated index labels.\n- Row order, index and the other columns are unchanged.\n\n**What I checked:**\n- On `tests/data/reports.csv`, A's first gap becomes 11.0 (its first later reading) and B's gap becomes 7.5 (its earlier reading).\n- A made-up frame with repeated index labels, a station with no temperatures and a row with no station gave the expected result in every case. The input was unchanged, and the index and other columns were identical to the input.\n\n**Needs your attention:** my check script `chk_tmp.py` is still in the project root because deleting it needed approval. You can delete it; nothing depends on it.\n\nI couldn't look up the pandas documentation (the Context7 quota was used up), so I relied on the tests and my own checks against pandas 3.0.6."
  },
